Source: Google Tsguide > Google TypeScript Style Guide > Language features > Control structures > Exception handling > Catching and rethrowing

##### Catching and rethrowing

When catching errors, code *should* assume that all thrown errors are instances
of `Error`.

```
function assertIsError(e: unknown): asserts e is Error {
 if (!(e instanceof Error)) throw new Error("e is not an Error");
}
try {
 doSomething();
} catch (e: unknown) {
 // All thrown errors must be Error subtypes. Do not handle
 // other possible values unless you know they are thrown.
 assertIsError(e);
 displayError(e.message);
 // or rethrow:
 throw e;
}
```
Exception handlers *must not* defensively handle non-`Error` types unless the
called API is conclusively known to throw non-`Error`s in violation of the above
rule. In that case, a comment should be included to specifically identify where
the non-`Error`s originate.

```
try {
 badApiThrowingStrings();
} catch (e: unknown) {
 // Note: bad API throws strings instead of errors.
 if (typeof e === 'string') { ... }
}
```
Why?

Avoid overly defensive programming. Repeating the same defenses against a problem that will not exist in most code leads to boiler-plate code that is not useful.

