Source: Eslint Rules > no-fallthrough > Rule Details

## Rule Details

This rule is aimed at eliminating unintentional fallthrough of one case to the other. As such, it flags any fallthrough scenarios that are not marked by a comment.

Examples of **incorrect** code for this rule:

```
/*eslint no-fallthrough: "error"*/
switch(foo) {
 case 1:
 doSomething();

}
```
Examples of **correct** code for this rule:

```
/*eslint no-fallthrough: "error"*/
switch(foo) {
 case 1:
 doSomething();
 break;
 case 2:
 doSomething();
}
function bar(foo) {
 switch(foo) {
 case 1:
 doSomething();
 return;
 case 2:
 doSomething();
 }
}
switch(foo) {
 case 1:
 doSomething();
 throw new Error("Boo!");
 case 2:
 doSomething();
}
switch(foo) {
 case 1:
 case 2:
 doSomething();
}
switch(foo) {
 case 1: case 2:
 doSomething();
}
switch(foo) {
 case 1:
 doSomething();
 // falls through
 case 2:
 doSomething();
}
switch(foo) {
 case 1: {
 doSomething();
 // falls through
 }
 case 2: {
 doSomethingElse();
 }
}
```
Note that the last `case` statement in these examples does not cause a warning because there is nothing to fall through into.

